course: physics
lesson: 41
title: Alice's fall and her sizes: falling, the centre of the Earth, giants and tiny people
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Alice falls down the rabbit hole so slowly that she takes a jar of orange marmalade off a shelf on the way and puts it back further down. In real life a fall gets faster and faster every second, so this only works in a dream, and the book is a dream.
explain: As she falls she wonders if she will come out on the other side of the Earth, among «the Antipathies» who walk with their heads downward. She is right that the Earth is about four thousand miles to its centre, but nobody on the other side is upside down: «down» everywhere means towards the middle of the Earth.
explain: Then she shrinks to ten inches and grows to over nine feet, and later to three inches and huge again. No drink or cake can do that, but asking «what would really happen if I were tiny, or a giant?» is real science: size changes how hot or cold you get, how heavy you are and how strong your legs need to be.
explain: So enjoy the dream, and keep the real rules for the real world: things fall faster and faster, the Earth's middle is hotter than any oven, and people grow slowly, a few centimetres a year.
fact: A falling object speeds up every second because gravity keeps pulling it; only air pushing back slows it, which is why a parachute falls gently and a stone does not.
fact: With no air in the way, a heavy thing and a light thing fall at the same speed; an astronaut showed this on the Moon in 1971 by dropping a hammer and a feather, which landed together.
fact: The distance to the centre of the Earth is about 6,400 kilometres, or about 4,000 miles, so Alice's guess of «four thousand miles down» was right.
fact: The Earth's centre is a ball of iron and nickel about as hot as the surface of the Sun, and around it is a layer of melted metal, so no tunnel could ever be dug through.
fact: In an imaginary airless tunnel through the Earth, you would speed up until the middle, then slow down and just reach the other side, then fall back again, like a swing.
fact: The antipodes of a place is the point exactly opposite it on the other side of the Earth; for Britain it is in the Pacific Ocean south-east of New Zealand, which is why people in Britain call Australia and New Zealand «the Antipodes».
fact: People in Australia and New Zealand stand with their feet down and heads up just like us, because «down» always means towards the centre of the Earth.
fact: Children usually grow about 5 to 6 centimetres a year, faster as babies and again during the teenage years, and stop growing in their late teens or early twenties.
fact: Food, sleep and exercise help children grow well, but no food or drink can make you shrink, or grow a lot in a few seconds as Alice does.
fact: A tiny person would lose heat quickly, like a mouse, because small bodies have a lot of skin for their size; that is why mice and shrews must eat very often to stay warm.
fact: If you were twice as tall, twice as wide and twice as thick, you would weigh about 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 times as much, but your bones would only be about 4 times as strong, so giants would find it very hard to stand and walk.
fact: A telescope like Alice's «largest telescope that ever was» was made of tubes that slide out of each other to make it longer; it makes far-away things look closer.
fact: When Alice cries as a giant she makes a pool of tears deep enough to swim in; real tears come in small drops, and even a long cry makes far less than a cupful.
exercise: What happens to the speed of a falling stone? | It keeps getting faster as it falls | It stays exactly the same all the way | It slows down as it gets nearer the ground | It stops halfway and floats for a while
exercise: Why does a parachute fall gently? | The air pushes back on it | Gravity does not pull on cloth | It is lighter than the air around it | The wind always blows it upwards
exercise: What landed first when a hammer and a feather were dropped on the Moon? | They landed together | The hammer, a long way first | The feather, floating down first | Neither, both floated off into space
exercise: About how far is it to the centre of the Earth? | About 6,400 kilometres | About 64 kilometres | About 640 kilometres | About 64,000 kilometres
exercise: Was Alice's guess of «four thousand miles down» right? | Yes, it is about right | No, it is only about four miles | No, it is about forty thousand miles | No, the Earth has no centre
exercise: What is the centre of the Earth like? | Hot metal, about as hot as the Sun's surface | Cold rock, frozen solid since the Earth began | A huge empty cave full of air | An ocean of water as cold as ice
exercise: Why can nobody dig a tunnel right through the Earth? | The middle is far too hot and partly melted | The Earth is hollow, so you would fall into a gap | The other side is upside down and the tunnel would flip | The rock is soft, so the tunnel would close behind you
exercise: In an imaginary airless tunnel through the Earth, where would you be going fastest? | At the centre | At the top of the tunnel | At the other side | Halfway to the centre
exercise: What are the antipodes of a place? | The point exactly opposite it through the Earth | The highest mountain in the same country | The nearest island across the sea | The two poles at the very top and the very bottom of a world map
exercise: Where are Britain's antipodes? | In the Pacific Ocean near New Zealand | In the middle of the Sahara Desert in North Africa | At the North Pole | In the Atlantic Ocean near America
exercise: Which way is «down» for a person in Australia? | Towards the centre of the Earth | Towards the North Pole | Towards the sky above them | Towards the Sun at noon
exercise: About how much does a child usually grow in a year? | About 5 to 6 centimetres | About 50 to 60 centimetres | About half a centimetre | About one metre
exercise: Can any food or drink make you shrink? | No, never | Yes, very cold drinks | Yes, if you eat mushrooms | Yes, but only at night
exercise: Why does a tiny animal like a mouse need to eat so often? | It loses heat fast because it has lots of skin for its size | It has a much bigger stomach than its body needs | It runs on sugar instead of air like we do, so it must refill all day long | It is always growing and never stops in its life
exercise: About how many times heavier would you be if you were twice as tall, wide and thick? | 8 times | 2 times | 4 times | 6 times
exercise: What does a telescope do? | Makes far-away things look closer | Makes small things grow bigger | Makes the person looking at it taller | Makes the dark sky lighter at night
test: «Alice fell so slow she grabbed some marmalade on the way, could I do that down a well?» What would really happen? | You would fall faster every second, far too fast to grab anything | You would fall slowly like Alice if the well was deep enough | You would stop halfway down and float in the middle | You would fall slowly if you held your breath and kept your arms out wide
test: A boy says, «heavy things always fall faster, everyone knows that». What does the Moon test show? | Without air, a hammer and a feather fall at the same speed | Heavy things fall faster everywhere, even on the Moon | Light things fall faster because air holds heavy things back | Nothing falls at all on the Moon because there is no air
test: Alice worries the people on the other side of the Earth walk «with their heads downward». What would they say about us? | Nothing odd: everyone stands with their feet towards the Earth's centre | That we are the ones walking upside down | That we all fall off the Earth at night | That we need to hold on to the ground with both hands to stop ourselves falling into the sky
test: «I'm gonna dig through to Australia from my back garden in Leeds» Why will that not work? | The middle of the Earth is hot melted metal, and the point opposite Britain is in the ocean anyway | You would come out in Australia but upside down, so you could not climb out | It would work, but it would take about a week with a good spade | You would get stuck in the middle because there is no up or down there, so you could not dig any further
test: In an imaginary airless tunnel through the Earth, what happens after you reach the far side? | You fall back down again, like a swing going to and fro | You float out into space and never come back | You stop at the far side and stay there forever | You come out and keep flying up into the sky
test: Your little brother drinks a whole bottle of milk and says, «watch me, I'm gonna get taller now». What is true? | Milk is good food, but growing takes months and years, not seconds | He will grow about a centimetre in the next hour | If he drinks two bottles he will grow twice as fast | Milk makes you grow, but only if you drink it upside down while standing on your head
test: When Alice is three inches tall, why would she get cold faster than a normal girl? | Small bodies have lots of skin for their size, so they lose heat quickly | Small bodies have no blood, so they cannot keep warm | The air is colder close to the ground everywhere | Small people cannot wear clothes of any kind, so their skin would be bare to the wind
test: Why would a real giant of nine times Alice's height have a terrible time standing up? | Its weight would grow far faster than the strength of its bones | Giants have no knees at all, so their legs would not bend | The air at that height has no oxygen in it at all | Giants are always too sleepy to stand up for long
test: «Alice cried a whole pool, could you really fill a bath with tears?» What is true? | No: even a long cry makes far less than a cupful | Yes: grown-ups cry about a bucket a day | Yes: if you cry for a whole afternoon | No: tears dry up before they reach the floor
test: The book ends with Alice waking up on the river bank. Why can the fall, the sizes and the pool of tears break the real rules of science? | Because it was all a dream | Because Wonderland is on the Moon | Because Alice was a magician | Because the rules were different in 1865
test: ★★★ A toy brick 1 cm long weighs 1 gram. A brick of the same shape and stuff, 3 cm long, 3 cm wide and 3 cm tall, weighs how much? | 27 grams | 9 grams | 3 grams | 6 grams
test: ★★★ Alice grows from 10 inches to 9 feet (108 inches). About how many times taller has she become? | About 11 times | About 9 times | About 100 times | About 2 times
exam: Identify the principal effect of gravitational attraction upon a body falling freely near the surface of the Earth. | Its velocity increases continually throughout the descent | Its velocity remains constant throughout the descent | Its velocity diminishes as the ground is approached | Its velocity becomes zero at the midpoint of the descent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the antipodes of a given location? | The point upon the Earth's surface diametrically opposite to it | The highest point of elevation within the same territory | The nearest island situated across the adjacent sea | The poles situated at the summit and base of a map
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the interior of the Earth. | Its central region consists of exceedingly hot metal, partly in a molten state | It consists of a hollow chamber filled with air | It consists of frozen rock of uniform temperature, unchanged since the formation of the planet | It consists of a subterranean ocean of fresh water
exam: Ascertain the approximate factor by which the weight of a body increases when each of its three dimensions is doubled. | Eight | Two | Four | Sixteen
